WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:04.880
"Cobra Kai" might be all about the karate wars,&nbsp;
but it has more in common with body slams and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:08.720
suplexes than anyone could have imagined.
One of the most monumental Terry Silver&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:13.360
scenes in "Cobra Kai" Season 4 is his ruthless&nbsp;
deal with rejected Cobra Kai student Stingray.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:17.760
After Silver beats Stingray up and sets up John&nbsp;
Kreese to take the fall, the millionaire alone is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:22.960
in control of the Cobra Kai dojo. In Season 5, we&nbsp;
find out that Stingray received bragging rights,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:26.800
Terry-enforced Cobra Kai respect, and a&nbsp;
sweet material compensation for his role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:30.800
in the betrayal. As the season proceeds,&nbsp;
it becomes clear that Stingray's cool new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:34.480
crib and even cooler new ride haven't been&nbsp;
able to extinguish the burning shame that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:38.720
hides underneath his confrontational demeanor.
Kreese might be in prison, but he's intent on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:43.040
proving that he's innocent -- and, as always, he&nbsp;
isn't above using underhanded tactics to achieve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:46.960
his goals. Knowing that Daniel LaRusso and his&nbsp;
allies wouldn't give him the time of day if he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:51.440
contacted them, Kreese enlists Tory Nichols to&nbsp;
give Daniel a nameless tip about the situation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:55.440
Desperate to get a win over Terry, Daniel&nbsp;
decides to pay Stingray a visit. When the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:59.360
controversial Cobra Kai student eventually makes&nbsp;
an appearance, he's behind the wheel of the cool&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:03.920
Cobra Kai Dodge Challenger that used to belong&nbsp;
to Johnny, rocking out to "Judas" by Fozzy.&nbsp;

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:08.400
The hard-rocking song about becoming a Judas is an&nbsp;
extremely good fit for the traitorous character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:13.440
who blasts it on full volume and sings along to&nbsp;
the lyrics. However, as it so often does, "Cobra&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:18.480
Kai" adds some seriously dark undercurrents and&nbsp;
deeper meanings in this deceptively simple scene.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:21.760
In fact, Stingray's new "theme song"&nbsp;
just so happens to mean much more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:25.200
you'd initially assume and alludes&nbsp;
to his rise and fall in the series.&nbsp;

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:29.360
Having the show's biggest Judas character rocking&nbsp;
out to a song called "Judas" is pretty obvious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:34.320
but there is a twist. The Judas in the song's&nbsp;
lyrics is absolutely horrified of what he's done,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:39.120
and hopes to find a way to right his wrongs. This&nbsp;
telegraphs Stingray's deep regret for his actions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:43.280
and his eventual redemption in the season&nbsp;
finale. As the chorus of the song goes:&nbsp;

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:46.800
"What have I become, now that I've&nbsp;
betrayed / Everyone I've ever loved,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:51.520
I pushed them all away / And I have been a slave&nbsp;
to the Judas in my mind / Is there something left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:55.600
of me to save in the wreckage of my life."
These few lines sum up Stingray's "Cobra&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:58.800
Kai" Season 5 arc in an eerily&nbsp;
efficient fashion, as he comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:02.960
to feel the exact same way about his actions.
Don't think that the song's hidden meanings end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:07.280
there, though. The singer of Fozzy is none other&nbsp;
than Chris Jericho, the famed pro wrestler who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:12.080
works with All Elite Wrestling, and uses "Judas"&nbsp;
as his entrance theme. This isn't Jericho's first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:16.560
time in the "Cobra Kai" limelight. The man himself&nbsp;
has thanked the show for a name drop in Season 3,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:21.840
Episode 6. Jericho also seems to be on good terms&nbsp;
with Martin Kove. The actor has appeared as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:26.640
guest on Jericho's "This Is Jericho" podcast,&nbsp;
and he's also turned up on "AEW Dynamite"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:30.640
as wrestler Dr. Britt Baker's sensei.
The lighter moments of "Cobra Kai" and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:35.360
professional wrestling scratch a pretty similar&nbsp;
itch, so they seem like a pretty natural match.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:39.200
Since "Cobra Kai" Season 5 only strengthens&nbsp;
Jericho's ties to the show, it could be just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:43.680
a matter of time before the hard-rocking wrestler&nbsp;
appears on the show in the flesh. Perhaps he could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:49.840
turn up as a rival sensei in the grand tournament&nbsp;
of the hopefully inevitable "Cobra Kai" Season 6?

